No `.csproj` file exists yet, so I can't determine the target framework. Since you said "class library" with no DI, the **lightest approach** depends on your .NET version:

### If .NET 8+ (recommended): use the built-in `TimeProvider`

No custom wrapper needed. Inject `TimeProvider` via the constructor:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider timeProvider)
{
    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => timeProvider.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

In production, pass `TimeProvider.System`. In tests, use `FakeTimeProvider`:

```csharp
// NuGet: Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 6, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(30));
Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

No DI container required — just pass `TimeProvider.System` at the call site.

### If pre-.NET 8: install the backport

```
dotnet add package Microsoft.Bcl.TimeProvider
```

Same API as above.

### Alternative: ambient `Clock` (zero constructor changes)

If you'd rather avoid touching every constructor, you can use an `AsyncLocal`-based ambient context — but constructor injection via `TimeProvider` is simpler, safer, and the .NET-recommended pattern. Want me to apply the `TimeProvider` change to `InvoiceCalculator.cs`?
